Type Good for Odoo relevance B-tree equality, ranges, sorting <code>index=True</code> on almost every field GIN membership in composite values: jsonb keys, arrays, full-text, trigrams trigram indexes for <code>ILIKE '%x%'</code>; <code>jsonb</code> search GiST geometric, ranges, nearest neighbour exclusion constraints on ranges BRIN huge append-only tables where the column correlates with physical order (timestamps) tiny index for log-like tables such as logs Hash equality only rarely worth it <p>Trigram indexes make <code>ILIKE '%term%'</code> fast. They need the <code>pg_trgm</code> extension:</p> <pre><code>-- fragment
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm;
CREATE INDEX res_partner_name_trgm_idx ON res_partner USING gin (name gin_trgm_ops);
</code></pre> <p>Versions. Odoo 16 gives a field <code>index=True</code> and creates a plain B-tree, named <code>&lt;table&gt;_&lt;field&gt;_index</code>. From Odoo 17 the <code>index</code> attribute also accepts <code>'btree'</code>, <code>'btree_not_null'</code> (index excluding NULLs) and <code>'trigram'</code> (GIN with <code>pg_trgm</code>; Odoo creates the extension when it can). Check your release's <code>fields.py</code> before relying on the extra values. In Odoo 16 to 18 translated fields are <code>jsonb</code>, so a trigram index on a translated <code>name</code> must target an extracted language key, not the raw column.</p>

ORM <code>write</code> SQL <code>UPDATE</code> Constraints, computes, overrides Run Skipped Speed on 1M rows Slow Fast Access rules Bypassed as superuser Bypassed Cache Consistent Stale, call <code>invalidate_all()</code> <p>Use the ORM by default. Use SQL when the logic is a plain value assignment and the volume makes the ORM impractical, then recompute dependants explicitly and check constraints yourself. Always run the SELECT with the same WHERE first:</p> <pre><code>SELECT count(*) FROM library_loan WHERE state = 'draft' AND create_date &lt; '2025-01-01';
UPDATE library_loan SET active = false WHERE state = 'draft' AND create_date &lt; '2025-01-01';
</code></pre>

View <code>groups</code> attribute Field <code>groups</code> attribute Where XML arch node Python field What it does removes the node for users outside the groups removes the field from the model for them Stops RPC reads no yes Stops reports, exports, search no yes (they use the ORM) Use for layout and convenience data protection <p>Use both: the field attribute protects the data, the view attribute keeps the form tidy for each audience and lets the same view serve groups with different access.</p> <pre><code>&lt;group string="Contact" groups="library.group_library_manager"&gt;
    &lt;field name="phone"/&gt;
    &lt;field name="internal_note"/&gt;
&lt;/group&gt;
</code></pre>
